Adapted from the skills of https://github.com/obra/superpowers (MIT, Copyright (c) 2025 Jesse
Vincent), read at version 6.3.0: `brainstorming` and `writing-plans` (incorporated into the canonical
Phase A and Phase B references of `skills/planning`, rather than shipped as overlapping skills),
`subagent-driven-development` together with `executing-plans` and the reviewer template of
`requesting-code-review` (folded into planning Phase C), `test-driven-development` with its
`writing-good-tests.md` (folded into planning's execution references),
`receiving-code-review` (folded into `/pr-review § 4.1`), and — folded into the shared references
that already stated the rule here — `using-superpowers`, `verification-before-completion` and
`dispatching-parallel-agents`.
`systematic-debugging` and its companion notes were compared against `skills/debugger`, which had
been written around them, and only what was missing was added there.
Rewritten for this harness: the three shell scripts of `subagent-driven-development` became one
Python standard-library script (`skills/planning/scripts/sdd.py`); the visual companion (a
Node server and its shell scripts) was dropped; every routine "commit" step became a working-tree
checkpoint that commits only with the user's approval in that turn; the model is chosen through the
agent's own frontmatter rather than passed at dispatch; spec and plan paths follow this plugin's
path conventions; the `dot` diagrams became prose and tables; and
`finishing-a-development-branch` was not ported, because the git rails here end at reviewed
working-tree changes.

  skills/intent-layer/

Adapted from the `intent-layer` skill of https://github.com/crafter-station/skills (MIT), by
Railly Hugo, itself built on Tyler Brandt's Intent Layer. The three shell scripts
(`detect_state.sh`, `analyze_structure.sh`, `estimate_tokens.sh`) were ported to one Python
standard-library script with a third subcommand, `check`, that upstream does not have — the gate
the setup playbook runs; the "only one root context file" rule was rewritten for the
`AGENTS.md` plus `CLAUDE.md` pair this harness installs, with `AGENTS.md` as the root node; the
root's `## Intent Layer` section became a row in the root's existing `Where things live` table;
the thresholds were kept and the Codex 32 KiB chain limit added. The capture protocol and the node
templates were rewritten around this harness's config placeholders and its rule that no value is
invented.

  skills/animate/

Adapted from four skills of https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills (MIT) — `animate`,
`review-animations`, `improve-animations` and `find-animation-opportunities` — together with the
`RECIPES.md`, `STANDARDS.md`, `AUDIT.md` and `PLAN-TEMPLATE.md` they carry. Merged into one skill
with four modes: the rulebook the four repeated lives once in SKILL.md, the shell sweeps became
`Grep` patterns, plans land in the project's `paths.planDir`, and the component-library and React
Native handoffs point at this harness's own agents rather than at the upstream sibling skills.

  skills/landing-page-design/

Adapted from the `landing-page-design` skill of https://github.com/elayadesign/ai-design-skills
(MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Elaya), forked from the upstream `main` file of 16,528 bytes as read on
2026-08-26. Part A — intake, section order, layout types, conversion and copy rules, build order,
the index decision, the ship floor — is kept in substance and rewritten for this harness. Part B,
the upstream visual canon, moved whole to `references/house-canon.md` as a fallback that yields to
the host project's design authority, the `designer` direction contract and the `animate` rulebook;
each value there carries its reason and is tagged craft or house taste. The upstream description,
which claimed every web UI, was narrowed to landing and marketing surfaces. Two upstream claims
were corrected against official 2026 sources — FAQ rich-result deprecation and current
`text-wrap: pretty` support — and one
rule was inverted: invented "organic" numbers are fabricated proof, so numbers are real or absent.

